{
  "_note": "EL GARAJE, as data. One record per vehicle, holding everything a designer would expect to change: how it drives, what it is made of, what it costs, how it sounds and what it LOOKS like. Four files used to own those five things between them — src/game/vehicles.js (stats), economy.js (price), audio.js (engine voice), and a 90-line if/else chain in c2d/entities.js (the art). What stays engine: the physics, the WebAudio synthesis, and the interpreter that walks `parts`.",
  "version": 1,
  "_geometry": "EVERY NUMBER IS IN THE VEHICLE'S OWN FRAME: centred at (0,0), facing +x, y down. A bare number is PIXELS. A pair [k, px] means k times the HALF-EXTENT of that value's own axis, plus px — so on x, [1,0] is the bow and [-1,3] is three pixels in from the transom; a width of [2,-6] is the full beam less six. That is the exact shape the hand-written art already had (`-veh.w/2 + 3`, `veh.h * 0.45`), which is why it can be transcribed without moving a pixel.",
  "_shapes": "The finite vocabulary the interpreter implements, and the only thing a part may ask for: rect, roundRect, ellipse, disc, poly (with quadratic segments), stripes, strokeRect, stroke. Same contract as the feria's shape DSL — data may compose these, never invent one.",
  "_silhouette": "A part marked `silhouette: true` ALSO joins the body outline that `traceVehicleSilhouette` traces for the ground shadow, and `silhouette: \"only\"` joins it without being painted. Deriving the shadow from the same list is the point: the sprite and the shadow used to be two hand-kept if/else chains, so a vehicle could be drawn with somebody else's shadow and only a screenshot would say.",
  "_colors": "`$color` and `$roof` resolve against the vehicle's own palette, which is what makes the shop's paint swatches work — economy.js clones the record with a new `color` and the art follows. Everything else is a literal, on purpose: the red boot-top and the white sheer ARE the boat idiom, and a swatch must not repaint them.",
